#{[17592186426805 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426805 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426805 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e893480f-5916-54cc-a5ac-b814cdf8c446" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426806 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426806 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426806 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ee78a4db-270a-5a24-94aa-7fd979241cf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426808 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426808 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426808 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426808 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426808 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426808 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426808 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426808 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186426806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426808 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1754e42f-ef75-5680-942c-adf751ac0ab2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426810 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426810 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426810 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426810 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426810 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186426805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426810 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426810 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426810 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186426805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426810 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3f81bcb6-7113-5591-b120-34386b2b4833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426812 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426812 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426812 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426812 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =8$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426812 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7043291-4c2d-5218-b2ec-199ed93eadcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426812 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-(-5)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=6,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426813 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426813 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80326" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426813 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426813 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =11$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426813 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba90f51b-352b-5787-ba63-3059791890ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186426813 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]-11[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-9{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429727 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429727 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 2 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1, 2]; waits [1, 1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (2/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 991->1049. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429727 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048742 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429728 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429728 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower we